WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:10.877
Hello, I'm Ron Vale and today I'd like to tell you about

00:00:10.878 --> 00:00:14.580
experiments that led to the discovery of a new type of

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:18.484
microtubule motor protein called kinesin. And in this

00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:21.487
talk, I'd like to highlight this experiment shown in this

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:26.759
video, where kinesins are attached onto a glass coverslip

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.264
and are transporting these microtubules across the glass.

00:00:31.265 --> 00:00:35.768
And I'll explain to you why this experiment came as a complete and

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:39.605
utter surprise to us. And also, how this experiment changed

00:00:39.606 --> 00:00:43.476
the course of the research project, but also changed the course

00:00:43.477 --> 00:00:48.581
of my life and career, as well. But before I go into these

00:00:48.582 --> 00:00:51.551
experiments, I'd just like to share some general thoughts with you

00:00:51.552 --> 00:00:56.522
about scientific discovery. We often teach that the way

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:59.292
scientific discoveries are made is through a process called

00:00:59.293 --> 00:01:02.895
the scientific method. And you can see many different versions of

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:07.767
them here, but they mostly reduce to a similar theme

00:01:07.768 --> 00:01:11.070
of first coming up with a question, then developing a

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:14.440
hypothesis, and then doing experiments to test that

00:01:14.441 --> 00:01:17.877
hypothesis, analyzing the data, and then coming to a

00:01:17.878 --> 00:01:23.583
conclusion. Now, many of the scientific projects I've been involved with

00:01:23.584 --> 00:01:27.386
haven't followed such a linear path and been so predictable.

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:32.525
Oftentimes, you get started with an idea, but then the experiments

00:01:32.526 --> 00:01:36.095
take you in all kinds of different directions. And there are

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:40.166
various twists and turns that happen in a project. And then maybe

00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:44.337
if you're very lucky, you come across in one of your experiments,

00:01:44.338 --> 00:01:50.243
some really unexpected result that tells you something very

00:01:50.244 --> 00:01:53.279
important and very new about biological systems.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.349
And I'd like to illustrate that with the story I'm going to

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:02.321
tell you. And this story, I think, is very much like a detective who

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:07.393
done it story. Where we started off following, in fact, the most obvious

00:02:07.394 --> 00:02:12.532
suspect, but only found out later that that suspect was not

00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:15.535
correct, and we were on the wrong track. So then we had to

00:02:15.536 --> 00:02:20.573
go back and find new clues, develop new pieces of evidence

00:02:20.574 --> 00:02:24.710
put things together in a new way that got us back on the right

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:28.514
track, and allowed us to come up with the correct answer.

00:02:28.515 --> 00:02:33.085
And indeed, many scientific projects are really like detective

00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:38.758
stories. So today, I'd like to share with you my detective story

00:02:38.759 --> 00:02:45.731
that started off when I was an MD PhD student in the lab of Eric Shooter.

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:51.971
And I was interested in this problem of how material is transported

00:02:51.972 --> 00:02:56.108
inside of nerve cells. In particular, I was studying this one hormone

00:02:56.109 --> 00:03:00.713
called nerve growth factor, which interacts with receptors

00:03:00.714 --> 00:03:04.817
at the nerve terminal and then some message must get

00:03:04.818 --> 00:03:09.555
transmitted back through this axon, back to this cell body.

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:15.595
But this axon, in fact, can be fantastically long. Even in a mature

00:03:15.596 --> 00:03:21.133
motor neuron in your body, the distal tip of that nerve terminal

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:25.805
may be at the tips of your toes, and that axon from that one nerve

00:03:25.806 --> 00:03:29.709
travels all the way up your leg, and then the cell body would

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:34.313
be in your spinal cord. So to transport material over this long

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:38.518
distance, there had to be some machinery that was moving material

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:43.956
inside the axon. But in 1983, very little was known about what

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:50.029
that machinery was. But an opportunity to begin to understand this

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:56.302
problem came about actually one floor below me at Stanford, in the

00:03:56.303 --> 00:04:00.406
laboratory of Jim Spudich, where a very exciting experiment was

00:04:00.407 --> 00:04:04.777
going on between Jim and a sabbatical professor, Mike Sheetz.

00:04:04.778 --> 00:04:10.082
And what Jim and Mike were trying to do was to develop

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:14.787
an in vitro assay to study muscle contraction. And what they did in their

00:04:14.788 --> 00:04:18.758
experiment was first of all to purify muscle myosin, and then

00:04:18.759 --> 00:04:24.397
add that purified muscle myosin onto plastic beads, and then they

00:04:24.398 --> 00:04:29.268
added these myosin-coated beads onto actin cables, which were

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:32.805
derived from a plant cell, Nitella. And you can see these beads

00:04:32.806 --> 00:04:37.810
or actually clumps of beads, being transported along these actin cables.

00:04:37.811 --> 00:04:41.747
And from watching these movies that I saw one floor below me downstairs,

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:46.419
it was very tempting to think that maybe a similar process

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:50.256
was going on in the axons. Perhaps myosin was coating the

00:04:50.257 --> 00:04:54.927
surface of membrane organelles and it was transporting these membrane

00:04:54.928 --> 00:05:00.333
organelles along actin filaments inside the axon. So Mike and I

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:04.637
wanted to test this idea with a very simple experiment.

00:05:04.638 --> 00:05:10.243
Which was to inject these myosin-coated beads inside of an

00:05:10.244 --> 00:05:13.079
axon and see if they would move. See if they would be transported

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.883
along actin filaments. So to do this, we had to find a good

00:05:16.884 --> 00:05:21.354
experimental organism. And we chose the granddaddy of

00:05:21.355 --> 00:05:26.192
all axons, which is the giant axon from the squid, which has an axon

00:05:26.193 --> 00:05:30.062
about 50 times bigger in diameter than a mammalian axon.

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:36.135
So the next step was to get some squid, so I called the Hopkins

00:05:36.136 --> 00:05:40.306
Marine Station in Monterey and asked them if they would be able to

00:05:40.307 --> 00:05:43.509
provide some squid for these experiments. And they said sure, we'd be very

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:47.246
happy to do that. We'll give you a call when we make a catch of

00:05:47.247 --> 00:05:52.818
squid. Well, weeks went by, in fact, months went by. And we never

00:05:52.819 --> 00:05:56.656
got a phone call from Hopkins Marine Station. So eventually, I called

00:05:56.657 --> 00:06:00.960
them in June and said, well, where's our squid? And they said,

00:06:00.961 --> 00:06:05.097
well, you know we've been trying to catch them but it's been a very unusual

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:08.301
year. We've been trying to fish for them and simply haven't been able

00:06:08.302 --> 00:06:13.105
to catch any. And indeed, that was an unusual year, as shown in this

00:06:13.106 --> 00:06:17.910
graph of the amount of squid that was caught in California

00:06:17.911 --> 00:06:24.383
over various years. And that year was 1983, where there was a precipitous

00:06:24.384 --> 00:06:28.554
decline in the amount of squid that were caught. And we now know why,

00:06:28.555 --> 00:06:35.228
1983 there was an unusual meteorological phenomenon called El Nino

00:06:35.229 --> 00:06:38.965
that warmed the coast of California. And when the water warmed,

00:06:38.966 --> 00:06:44.570
the squid just disappeared. So this seemed like incredibly bad luck,

00:06:44.571 --> 00:06:48.975
however, this bad luck turned into good luck in the end, as I'll

00:06:48.976 --> 00:06:54.647
describe to you. And in fact, it's funny to think but perhaps, a lot of

00:06:54.648 --> 00:06:59.318
the luck that I've had in my scientific career is due to this El Nino

00:06:59.319 --> 00:07:03.623
phenomenon. Because what El Nino did was to force us to change our

00:07:03.624 --> 00:07:08.127
plans and move and do the experimental work at this

00:07:08.128 --> 00:07:12.298
beautiful location, which is the Marine Biological Laboratory

00:07:12.299 --> 00:07:17.970
at Woods Hole. Now, not only was there a lot of squid at

00:07:17.971 --> 00:07:21.908
the MBL in Massachusetts, but there was also incredibly

00:07:21.909 --> 00:07:25.978
exciting research going on at the time. Indeed an entire new

00:07:25.979 --> 00:07:30.516
revolution in microscopy that was developed independently

00:07:30.517 --> 00:07:35.388
by Robert Allen and Shinya Inoue. And what these scientists did

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:39.792
was instead of looking through the microscope with your eyes,

00:07:39.793 --> 00:07:43.663
they attached a video camera and also used computers to

00:07:43.664 --> 00:07:47.366
enhance the contrast of these images and saw details

00:07:47.367 --> 00:07:51.037
inside of cells that were never seen before by microscopy.

00:07:51.038 --> 00:07:56.442
And Allen, Brady, and Lasek used this kind of microscopy technique

00:07:56.443 --> 00:08:01.247
to investigate axonal transport in squid. And they made these

00:08:01.248 --> 00:08:04.584
amazing movies, such as shown here, where you can see

00:08:04.585 --> 00:08:09.722
now all of these fine details of small organelles being transported

00:08:09.723 --> 00:08:14.594
up and down in this axon. So for the first time, axonal transport

00:08:14.595 --> 00:08:20.166
could be directly visualized. So this was all very exciting, but again,

00:08:20.167 --> 00:08:24.504
we had some bad news right when we arrived at Woods Hole.

00:08:24.505 --> 00:08:29.675
And I opened up this issue of Nature, and found out to my great surprise

00:08:29.676 --> 00:08:32.912
that someone else did the experiment that we wanted to do.

00:08:32.913 --> 00:08:38.084
They took foreign particles, which were in fact beads, small plastic

00:08:38.085 --> 00:08:42.522
beads, and microinjected them into an axon and found

00:08:42.523 --> 00:08:47.493
that they were rapidly transported inside the axon. Except it

00:08:47.494 --> 00:08:51.664
wasn't exactly the experiment that we wanted to do, because these were not

00:08:51.665 --> 00:08:54.300
myosin-coated beads, these were inert beads that had nothing

00:08:54.301 --> 00:08:58.638
on them. And in fact, this was a mystery. The investigators

00:08:58.639 --> 00:09:01.574
themselves couldn't explain it. They thought, well maybe there

00:09:01.575 --> 00:09:06.245
was a flow of fluid going on inside the axon and these beads got

00:09:06.246 --> 00:09:10.716
caught up in this fluid flow. Or perhaps myosin got picked up by

00:09:10.717 --> 00:09:14.387
these beads. Or maybe they were just piggybacking on membrane

00:09:14.388 --> 00:09:18.691
organelles. So they didn't have a good idea of why this happened.

00:09:18.692 --> 00:09:23.596
Undaunted, we went ahead and did our experiment with myosin-coated

00:09:23.597 --> 00:09:28.467
beads. So we took our myosin-coated beads, added it to the squid axoplasm,

00:09:28.468 --> 00:09:32.104
and the moment of truth came, and we saw nothing.

00:09:32.105 --> 00:09:38.744
These myosin-coated beads did not move at all, so that was disappointing.

00:09:38.745 --> 00:09:42.381
We then still had these control beads that didn't have any myosin

00:09:42.382 --> 00:09:47.353
on them, and tried them. And surprisingly, these beads moved.

00:09:47.354 --> 00:09:51.090
So we got the same strange result that these other investigators

00:09:51.091 --> 00:09:55.995
did, which we couldn't explain either. But what we did know

00:09:55.996 --> 00:10:00.933
was that our original idea of these myosin-coated beads

00:10:00.934 --> 00:10:05.004
finding and moving along actin cables in the squid axon

00:10:05.005 --> 00:10:08.608
was somehow incorrect. So we had to go back to the drawing board and

00:10:08.609 --> 00:10:13.613
develop a new plan of investigation. And Mike and I did that by

00:10:13.614 --> 00:10:18.751
joining up with Tom Reese and Bruce Schnapp. Tom had a

00:10:18.752 --> 00:10:22.922
laboratory in Woods Hole, and both Tom and Bruce were

00:10:22.923 --> 00:10:26.025
exceptional electron microscopists. Bruce also developed

00:10:26.026 --> 00:10:30.930
a video microscope in Tom's lab. And the four of us worked as

00:10:30.931 --> 00:10:34.100
a team to do all the experiments that I'm going to describe to you

00:10:34.101 --> 00:10:40.973
next. So first of all, to probe this system deeper, we took

00:10:40.974 --> 00:10:45.378
the inside of the squid axon out, the axoplasm, and kind of broke it

00:10:45.379 --> 00:10:50.082
apart. And then found out in this dissociated axoplasm, transport

00:10:50.083 --> 00:10:53.286
still continued. But now you could see that there were individual

00:10:53.287 --> 00:10:58.024
tracks that were supporting this axonal transport.

00:10:58.025 --> 00:11:01.027
However, with the lower resolution of this video microscope,

00:11:01.028 --> 00:11:05.598
we didn't know what these tracks were. So we had to go to the electron

00:11:05.599 --> 00:11:10.670
microscope to get that detail. And Tom and Bruce did this brilliant

00:11:10.671 --> 00:11:14.273
experiment where they first looked at a field of view under the

00:11:14.274 --> 00:11:17.610
video microscope and then found the same field of view under an

00:11:17.611 --> 00:11:21.614
electron microscope, to find out what these filaments were. And then

00:11:21.615 --> 00:11:24.250
indeed, when they went to the electron microscope, they found

00:11:24.251 --> 00:11:27.787
organelles on single filaments that had the diameter

00:11:27.788 --> 00:11:32.358
and the features of microtubules. So the main tracks of axonal

00:11:32.359 --> 00:11:35.895
transport, the long distance tracks, were not actin. They were microtubules.

00:11:35.896 --> 00:11:41.434
But the question then was, well the motor could be myosin, which

00:11:41.435 --> 00:11:45.905
only worked on actin. So some other motor had to be at play here.

00:11:45.906 --> 00:11:49.542
And we originally had some hypotheses for what that motor would

00:11:49.543 --> 00:11:54.080
be. But those hypotheses also turned out to be wrong. So we had to take an

00:11:54.081 --> 00:11:59.051
unbiased approach to understand the motor and the machinery that was

00:11:59.052 --> 00:12:03.422
driving this transport. So one way to take this approach was

00:12:03.423 --> 00:12:06.759
to reconstitute this system, essentially take it apart, put it back

00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:09.962
together again, and find out what was the essential machinery

00:12:09.963 --> 00:12:15.168
and perhaps the motor. So we did this by introducing tracks, which

00:12:15.169 --> 00:12:19.505
were here made from purified tubulin. We then also took

00:12:19.506 --> 00:12:23.543
the cargo, which were the membrane organelles, purified from

00:12:23.544 --> 00:12:27.513
the squid giant axon, and added ATP as an energy source.

00:12:27.514 --> 00:12:30.483
And we figured that the motors were going to be bound to

00:12:30.484 --> 00:12:34.820
these membrane organelles, and when they had ATP they would move along

00:12:34.821 --> 00:12:39.325
these microtubules. But again, disappointingly, the experiment

00:12:39.326 --> 00:12:43.729
was negative. We didn't see any movement. Well then

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:46.999
we rationalized, okay maybe something is missing here.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.337
Something else is needed. So we added to this mix a soluble

00:12:51.338 --> 00:12:54.507
protein fraction containing hundreds of different kinds of proteins.

00:12:54.508 --> 00:13:00.213
And to our joy, this worked, we now saw lots of membranes moving along these

00:13:00.214 --> 00:13:04.283
microtubule tracks. And this happened late at night, but before

00:13:04.284 --> 00:13:08.387
going home, we decided to do what was sort of a boring control

00:13:08.388 --> 00:13:13.326
experiment, just to make sure that the things that we saw that were moving were

00:13:13.327 --> 00:13:16.696
not particles coming from this soluble protein fraction, but in fact

00:13:16.697 --> 00:13:21.467
were indeed the organelles. We mixed everything together

00:13:21.468 --> 00:13:25.338
but now without the organelles. So we just added the microtubules,

00:13:25.339 --> 00:13:29.275
ATP, and the soluble protein fraction, expecting nothing to

00:13:29.276 --> 00:13:33.813
happen at all. Well, what happened blew our mind. What we saw

00:13:33.814 --> 00:13:38.184
under the microscope was that these microtubules were attaching

00:13:38.185 --> 00:13:41.521
themselves onto the glass coverslip and they were crawling

00:13:41.522 --> 00:13:46.359
across the surface of the glass like little worms. And this was,

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:50.129
as I said, completely unexpected. We had no anticipation

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:52.832
that this was going to happen at all. But then in thinking

00:13:52.833 --> 00:13:56.702
about this in more detail, what we thought was happening

00:13:56.703 --> 00:14:00.806
here was that in this soluble protein fraction, there were motor

00:14:00.807 --> 00:14:04.243
proteins that were just floating around in the cytosol. And

00:14:04.244 --> 00:14:08.347
they attached onto the glass surface and they were grabbing hold

00:14:08.348 --> 00:14:12.084
of these microtubules and then transporting these microtubules across

00:14:12.085 --> 00:14:17.056
the glass surface. So suddenly, this old experiment that we did

00:14:17.057 --> 00:14:20.126
with these control beads made sense. Maybe these control beads

00:14:20.127 --> 00:14:25.097
were picking up this soluble motor protein and then moving along

00:14:25.098 --> 00:14:29.001
these microtubule tracks. So we now tested this again

00:14:29.002 --> 00:14:32.572
using our soluble protein fraction, and indeed, these

00:14:32.573 --> 00:14:36.776
beads picked up a motor from the soluble protein mix and

00:14:36.777 --> 00:14:41.480
were moving beautifully along these microtubules. So why was this

00:14:41.481 --> 00:14:45.218
a surprise? Well, the reason is that most people in the field,

00:14:45.219 --> 00:14:48.588
including us, thought that the motor for this transport would

00:14:48.589 --> 00:14:52.491
be permanently bound either to the membrane organelles, which were

00:14:52.492 --> 00:14:56.996
the cargo, or onto the filamentous track like the microtubule. But no one

00:14:56.997 --> 00:15:01.400
expected there to be a lot of soluble motor floating around

00:15:01.401 --> 00:15:05.605
in the cytoplasm. But the fact that there was a lot of soluble motor

00:15:05.606 --> 00:15:09.976
gave us an opportunity to think about now purifying the motor

00:15:09.977 --> 00:15:14.747
protein from this complex mixture of proteins. The problem was

00:15:14.748 --> 00:15:18.618
this experiment happened at the very end of August, and I

00:15:18.619 --> 00:15:21.921
was supposed to go back to medical school in two weeks

00:15:21.922 --> 00:15:26.592
and start my clerkships. And it would probably be another three years

00:15:26.593 --> 00:15:30.363
until I was going to get back into research again. So I called up Stanford

00:15:30.364 --> 00:15:32.865
and I said, I had a very exciting experiment and I needed to keep

00:15:32.866 --> 00:15:36.736
working on it, can I postpone my medical school clerkships?

00:15:36.737 --> 00:15:43.676
And they said yes. So that fall and winter, I spent trying to purify

00:15:43.677 --> 00:15:48.781
this motor protein. Now, Woods Hole is a very exciting place, lots of

00:15:48.782 --> 00:15:52.818
people there in the summer. And in particular, in the '80s, there

00:15:52.819 --> 00:15:58.524
was very few people in the winter. So I kind of had this interesting

00:15:58.525 --> 00:16:03.596
monastic environment of just really trying to focus on this problem

00:16:03.597 --> 00:16:07.300
of purifying the motor. And of course, it was really one of the most rewarding

00:16:07.301 --> 00:16:12.538
and exciting times of my life. So the experiment really was to use this

00:16:12.539 --> 00:16:16.642
motility assay in combination with protein purification techniques

00:16:16.643 --> 00:16:21.614
to identify which protein of these hundreds of proteins that were in this

00:16:21.615 --> 00:16:26.118
soluble fraction was the motor. So you could use this motility

00:16:26.119 --> 00:16:30.223
assay for this purpose. So here are different fractions coming

00:16:30.224 --> 00:16:34.327
off of a protein column, and here, each of these fractions now

00:16:34.328 --> 00:16:37.730
is assayed for motor activity. And you can see this peak of

00:16:37.731 --> 00:16:44.003
microtubule motor activity here corresponding to this polypeptide

00:16:44.004 --> 00:16:47.573
which had never been described before. And since it was a new

00:16:47.574 --> 00:16:50.576
type of protein, a new type of motor, we needed a name.

00:16:50.577 --> 00:16:52.979
And a lot of people ask me where did the name kinesin

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:57.984
come from? Well, I could tell you that it came from a phone call with my mother.

00:16:57.985 --> 00:17:03.489
And indeed, my mother from the Greek root "kine" came up with

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:10.096
the name kinesin. Since 1985, it's also been amazing to see

00:17:10.097 --> 00:17:13.599
how the whole field of kinesin has developed. In 1985, we knew

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:17.803
almost nothing about how this motor moved objects along

00:17:17.804 --> 00:17:21.374
microtubules. But since that time, single molecule assays

00:17:21.375 --> 00:17:25.378
were developed. It was possible to get X-ray and EM structures

00:17:25.379 --> 00:17:29.482
of kinesin. All kinds of protein engineering experiments. You know these

00:17:29.483 --> 00:17:31.918
things were done in our lab and many laboratories around the world.

00:17:31.919 --> 00:17:35.288
Now we actually have a pretty good idea of how this motor

00:17:35.289 --> 00:17:40.426
is actually moving along a microtubule track. The same thing happened

00:17:40.427 --> 00:17:44.530
in biology. At the beginning, in 1985, we had one kinesin motor

00:17:44.531 --> 00:17:48.201
but since then, many kinesin motors have been discovered.

00:17:48.202 --> 00:17:52.505
It's now known to be a large superfamily. They're known to be

00:17:52.506 --> 00:17:57.076
involved in many different roles in cell biology, and several

00:17:57.077 --> 00:18:03.249
neurodegenerative diseases have been linked now to mutations in

00:18:03.250 --> 00:18:07.286
kinesin motors as well. So it's been a remarkable adventure,

00:18:07.287 --> 00:18:11.157
a remarkable journey. This detective story that I told you

00:18:11.158 --> 00:18:15.795
about had moments of frustration when we couldn't get squid,

00:18:15.796 --> 00:18:19.232
we had times when we had results where we simply couldn't

00:18:19.233 --> 00:18:23.402
explain, but there were also these fantastic eureka moments that

00:18:23.403 --> 00:18:27.373
you really will remember for the rest of your life. And also

00:18:27.374 --> 00:18:31.010
an opportunity to really dig into a problem and emerge with

00:18:31.011 --> 00:18:36.182
the satisfying mechanism. Now what about my career in

00:18:36.183 --> 00:18:40.253
medicine? I never did go back to medical school, I never did

00:18:40.254 --> 00:18:44.524
complete my clerkships. And that was all for the best, I've

00:18:44.525 --> 00:18:50.963
enjoyed being a scientific detective and I've enjoyed all the

00:18:50.964 --> 00:18:54.300
problems I've worked on. And I hope you will enjoy your

00:18:54.301 --> 00:19:15.489
scientific detective work as well. Thank you.

